      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the ultimate goal for some powerful corporations was indeed the absolute defense of profit, the resistance to union power often involved legitimate, albeit often aggressive, arguments rooted in the ideology of free-market capitalism.\n\nPro-corporate arguments often centered on the belief that rigid union demands would stifle necessary economic growth, increase the cost of doing business, and potentially make the nation uncompetitive in the global market.\n\nHowever, in political reality, these \"pro-market\" arguments were frequently used to defend the status and power of established capital, often at the direct and severe political and economic cost of the very working people the system claimed to support.",
